findings.body:
`newResetToken()` now builds a password-reset token from `Math.random()` and a 10^12 numeric range. That is a cryptographically weak PRNG with at most ~40 bits of entropy (8–9 base-36 characters after `padStart(9, "0")`), versus the previous 256-bit `randomBytes(32)` value.

If an attacker can request resets or guess tokens, they can brute-force or predict a valid token and take over the account. `Math.random` is also recoverable from observed outputs in common engines (e.g. V8 XorShift128+).

Use the Web Crypto CSPRNG, which is available on edge runtimes:

```suggestion
  const bytes = new Uint8Array(32);
  crypto.getRandomValues(bytes);
  return Array.from(bytes, (b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, "0")).join("");
```
